Under NC Medicaid’s **Orthotics & Prosthetics** policy, the specifically **not covered** items are certain devices or supplies that fall into excluded categories, and coverage generally requires a valid order, medical necessity, recent face-to-face evaluation, and ongoing review. [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment)]

## Specifically not covered

### Item-specific exclusions
NC Medicaid does **not** cover the following under Policy 5B:

- Devices for the beneficiary’s **comfort or convenience**, or for the convenience of the beneficiary’s caregiver(s)
- **Backup devices** or duplicate devices kept at different locations
- Devices and supplies for **residents of nursing facilities**
- Devices or supplies **covered by another agency**
- Equipment or supplies for beneficiaries **receiving hospice care** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 4.2.1]

### General noncoverage rules
A service, product, or procedure is also not covered if:

- the beneficiary does not meet the **eligibility requirements** in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does not meet the **coverage criteria** in Section 3.0;
- it **duplicates** another provider’s procedure, product, or service; or
- it is **experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 4.1]

## General criteria that must be met for coverage

For orthotic or prosthetic devices to be covered, the policy requires that they:

- be **ordered by a physician, physician assistant, or nurse practitioner**;
- be **medically necessary** to maintain or improve the beneficiary’s medical, physical, or functional level;
- be **appropriate for use in a noninstitutional setting** where normal life activities take place;
- be supported by a **documented face-to-face encounter** with the ordering physician, PA, or NP **no more than six months before** initiation of the orthotic or prosthetic;
- have the beneficiary’s need **reviewed at least annually** by the ordering physician, PA, or NP;
- be a **reasonable and medically necessary part of the treatment plan**;
- be **consistent with the beneficiary’s diagnosis and medical condition**, especially functional limitations and symptoms; and
- be of **high quality**, with replacement parts available and obtainable [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), §§ 3.2, 3.2.1]

## Additional medical-necessity standards by device type

- **Orthotic devices** are medically necessary when required to **correct or prevent skeletal deformities**, **support or align movable body parts**, or **preserve or improve physical function**.
- **Prosthetic devices** are medically necessary when they **replace all or part of the function** of a permanently inoperative, absent, or malfunctioning body part, and the beneficiary must require the prosthesis for **mobility, daily care, or rehabilitation purposes** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 3.2.1]

## Documentation-related criteria
The policy also states, in Attachment E, that the requested device must:

- fit the **definition of an orthotic or prosthetic**;
- be **safe, effective, and economical**;
- **not** be intended for the convenience of the beneficiary, caregiver, or provider;
- be **medical in nature** and generally recognized as an accepted method of treatment; and
- **not** be experimental or investigational [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), Attachment E]

I checked for amendments to Policy 5B in the corpus and found no listed change notices; the authoritative document returned was **Orthotics & Prosthetics (Policy 5B)**.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and person-centered service plan, and they must be aimed at improving the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. The beneficiary also agrees to work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, and technology. Current governing source: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) (effective Sept. 1, 2025; no amendment was returned for this document in the amendment check).

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA covers equipment, modification, and technology when the service is intended to:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve environmental and community accessibility; or
- address the beneficiary’s 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.4.5.

The policy also says the beneficiary agrees to:

- work collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education, and goods and services.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “I agree to:”.

### Types of home modifications listed
The policy’s equipment/modification/technology section includes examples such as:

- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- bathroom modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- hypoallergenic filters in qualifying cases;
- lifts and certain related accessibility devices; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed or damaged during an approved modification.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY”.

### Residence-related limits
For home modifications, the policy text supports coverage in:

- a primary private residence where the beneficiary lives and that is owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence when the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence when the owner allows the modification.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY”.

### Important exclusions/limits
The policy excludes or limits items such as:

- items not of direct medical or remedial benefit;
- items covered under the Medicaid State Plan DME/orthotics/prosthetics/home health benefit;
- recreational items;
- items of general utility to a non-disabled person;
- warranties and unrelated service agreements/maintenance contracts; and
- replacement of equipment that was improperly used, lost, or purposely damaged.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “Limits, Amount And Frequency”.

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment/home modification requests, the policy requires documentation showing the beneficiary’s need and supporting the request. Specifically, the MDT documentation must include:

- **a recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s needs for the assistive technology, equipment, or home modification requested;
- **the cost of the item** the beneficiary requires;
- **a statement of need identified in the service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- **other information required for the specific item requested**;
- **quotes**, when required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number NC Medicaid requires; and
- any **written recommendations from the professional(s)** NC Medicaid or its designee determines are appropriate in special circumstances.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1.

## Additional documentation requirements tied to the request
The policy also identifies supporting documentation that must be part of the request record:

- a **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment** and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the item/modification was provided and the cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP business system.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “Limits, Amount And Frequency”.

## Training/documentation requirement
If the request involves equipment or technology, the policy also requires:

- a plan for training the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment; and
- documentation by the case manager that the training was completed, signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party.  
  Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1.

