Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet the certification standards in Policy 12B and maintain them on an ongoing basis. A provider can be decertified for a wide range of operational, staffing, training, documentation, billing, confidentiality, and compliance failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid Policy 12B says an HIV Case Management provider must:

- have a documented successful record of **3 years** providing or managing HIV Case Management programs;
- ensure HIV Case Management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting Policy 12B staff criteria;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting Policy 12B supervisor criteria;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as a provider of HIV Case Management services;
- meet all applicable **state and federal laws** governing Medicaid participation;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a **quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days** of the proposed change, and no later than **5 business days** after the actual change; and
- achieve **national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting agencies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (Policy 12B, § 6.2.1, effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check)

The recertification checklist adds practical submission requirements. It asks, among other things, whether the recertification package includes:

- only **one physical location** per application;
- a **signed Recertification Application Checklist**;
- all case managers and supervisors with **dates of employment**;
- all counties where HIV case management services are provided;
- agency services offered;
- **operating hours** and an **after-hours emergency plan**;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current per amendment check)

Policy 12B’s recertification section also requires the provider to:

- submit a **complete and signed recertification application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of items from the initial certification materials that have changed;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- undergo a **recertification on-site visit**, including record and business documentation review as needed. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (Policy 12B, § 6.2.4, effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check)

## Grounds for decertification

Policy 12B, § 6.2.5 states a provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide core service components;
- fraudulent billing practices;
- owner conviction of a felony;
- failure within **30 calendar days** to develop, submit, and implement a written plan of correction, or to make required corrections, for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- falsification of records;
- violation of beneficiary confidentiality;
- employing staff who do not meet Policy 12B staff criteria;
- failure of staff to complete required basic training within **90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required continuing education units;
- failure to provide adequate staff supervision to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within policy or agency deadlines;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, including documentation required by § 7.5;
- failure to implement and enforce a quality improvement program;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of changes in agency name, director/ownership, mailing address, or phone numbers, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable federal and state laws, regulations, Medicaid plan requirements, and governing policies;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (Policy 12B, § 6.2.5, effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check)

## Bottom line

In short, certification requires proven program experience, qualified staff and supervision, site-by-site Medicaid enrollment, legal compliance, quality improvement oversight, record access for review, timely notice of business changes, and timely national accreditation. Decertification can occur if the provider stops meeting those standards or commits serious compliance failures such as billing fraud, falsified records, confidentiality violations, staffing or training deficiencies, inadequate documentation, or failure to correct cited problems.

